<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  macOS entitlements.

  WHY THIS FILE EXISTS, and why its absence was invisible:

  Notarization REQUIRES the hardened runtime, and Tauri turns it on by default
  (`bundle.macOS.hardenedRuntime` defaults to true). Under the hardened runtime, a
  process may not open an audio input device unless it carries the
  `com.apple.security.device.audio-input` entitlement — the request is killed by TCC,
  not refused politely.

  So Relay would have shipped like this:

    tauri dev            → microphone works   (no hardened runtime)
    unsigned pre-release → microphone works   (ad-hoc signed, no hardened runtime)
    SIGNED + NOTARIZED   → microphone DEAD

  In other words, the FIRST build that is correct enough to hand to a church is the
  first one where Relay cannot hear the preacher — and no amount of testing the
  builds we could actually make would have revealed it. The app would have looked
  perfect, started listening, and transcribed nothing, forever.

  Relay is an app whose entire reason to exist is that it hears the sermon. This one
  key is the difference between that being true and being false.

  Nothing else is granted. Relay is not sandboxed, so no network or file entitlements
  are needed, and library validation stays ON (whisper.cpp is statically linked — we
  load no unsigned dylibs and must not weaken the hardening to pretend otherwise).
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
